WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.672 --> 00:00:13.082
"Here is sixty-thousand dollars," said Mr.

2
00:00:13.122 --> 00:00:16.284
Fogg, emptying his carpetbag almost to the last of his money.

3
00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:22.249
The Henrietta was a fine ship but twenty years old, and this price was easily double its worth.

4
00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:26.693
The ship's deck and cabins were made of wood, but its hull was of iron.

5
00:00:26.713 --> 00:00:32.092
"I will keep the iron hull and the engine," said Captain Speedy.

6
00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:34.793
He seized the bank notes and counted them.

7
00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:40.134
Mr. Fogg's first command as the Henrietta's new owner was to pull the boat apart.

8
00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:45.336
The rails, bunks, masts, and life rafts were immediately fed into the boilers.

9
00:00:46.716 --> 00:00:51.818
The next day, with Passepartout joining in, the crew pulled down the bridge and the cabins and began

10
00:00:51.858 --> 00:00:53.078
to tear up the very deck.

11
00:00:55.198 --> 00:01:00.180
On December 20, as it approached the coast of Ireland, the Henrietta was nothing more than a flat

12
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.863
hulk. By ten o'clock in the evening, the last possible sliver of wood had gone into the fire,

13
00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:08.565
but the boat was still 24 hours from Liverpool.

14
00:01:08.585 --> 00:01:12.828
"Everything is against you," Captain Speedy said to Mr.

15
00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:16.110
Fogg. "You have only reached the harbor of Queenstown."

16
00:01:17.071 --> 00:01:21.954
Mr. Fogg knew that Queenstown was the Irish port where the transatlantic steamers dropped off the mail.

17
00:01:22.674 --> 00:01:26.777
Trains carried the mail to Dublin, and from there fast boats delivered it to Liverpool.

18
00:01:28.318 --> 00:01:30.860
This way the mail arrived 12 hours ahead of the steamers.

19
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.981
Relying on the Henrietta's last puffs of steam, Mr.

20
00:01:35.021 --> 00:01:37.562
Fogg directed the boat toward the harbor lights on the shore.

21
00:01:38.403 --> 00:01:41.464
The Henrietta entered Queenstown at one o'clock in the morning.

22
00:01:42.364 --> 00:01:47.446
Phileas Fogg, leaving Captain Speedy on the iron hulk of his boat, went ashore with his party at

23
00:01:47.566 --> 00:01:54.749
once. They took the express train to Dublin and then crossed the Irish Sea, arriving in Liverpool at

24
00:01:54.849 --> 00:01:56.230
noon on December 21.

25
00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.291
He was only six hours distant from London.

26
00:02:00.131 --> 00:02:02.036
The wager would be won!

27
00:02:02.056 --> 00:02:04.098
At that moment Fix stepped up beside Mr.

28
00:02:04.138 --> 00:02:09.943
Fogg, put his hand on his shoulder, and showing the warrant, said, "I arrest you, Phileas Fogg, in

29
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:11.063
the Queen's name!"

30
00:02:12.244 --> 00:02:14.546
Ten minutes later Phileas Fogg was in prison.

31
00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:20.031
Aouda and Passepartout sat in the waiting room of the police station, not willing to leave.

32
00:02:21.252 --> 00:02:27.297
Passepartout, when he had seen his master arrested, had wanted to attack Fix, but a second policeman had

33
00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:27.917
held him back.

34
00:02:29.158 --> 00:02:31.020
Aouda was thunderstruck at the sudden event.

35
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.044
When Passepartout explained the situation to her, her heart refused to believe the wicked charge against her protector.

36
00:02:39.304 --> 00:02:40.265
Realizing that Mr.

37
00:02:40.305 --> 00:02:44.688
Fogg was now in need of saving and she could do nothing to help, Aouda cried bitterly.

38
00:02:45.808 --> 00:02:48.090
Passepartout cried as well, hanging his head.

39
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:52.652
"If only I had not concealed Fix's errand," he said bitterly.

40
00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:55.914
"If I had revealed Fix's true purpose, Mr.

41
00:02:55.974 --> 00:02:58.336
Fogg could have proven his innocence long before today!"

42
00:02:59.568 --> 00:03:00.148
As for Mr.

43
00:03:00.188 --> 00:03:01.749
Fix, he had arrested Mr.

44
00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:03.049
Fogg because it was his duty.

45
00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:04.950
The matter was simple.

46
00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.750
He was a police detective with a warrant for Mr.

47
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:08.431
Fogg's arrest.

48
00:03:09.211 --> 00:03:11.631
Mr. Fogg's guilt or innocence was not his concern.

49
00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:17.173
The arrested man sat in his cell, motionless, calm, and without apparent anger.

50
00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:22.274
He carefully removed his watch, set it on the table, and observed its advancing hands.

51
00:03:23.335 --> 00:03:25.675
The door was locked and the window heavily barred.

52
00:03:26.516 --> 00:03:27.796
The situation was terrible.

53
00:03:28.724 --> 00:03:32.706
If Phileas Fogg was honest, he was ruined; and if he was a thief, he was caught!

54
00:03:33.566 --> 00:03:40.409
He pulled his journal and pen from his pocket and wrote, "December 21, Saturday, Liverpool, 80th day, 12:20

55
00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:42.730
p.m." He waited, but for what?

56
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:44.511
Did he still hold out hope?

57
00:03:45.231 --> 00:03:46.311
Was he planning an escape?

58
00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:47.792
No one could tell.

59
00:03:49.293 --> 00:03:51.534
Two hours passed, and then half an hour more.

60
00:03:52.394 --> 00:03:55.935
He heard a noise outside—quick steps, and then a hasty opening of doors.

61
00:03:57.636 --> 00:04:01.600
Passepartout's voice was audible and immediately after, so was the voice of Fix.

62
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.064
The cell door swung open, and Passepartout, Aouda, and Fix hurried toward Fogg.

63
00:04:08.666 --> 00:04:09.527
Fix was out of breath.

64
00:04:11.068 --> 00:04:17.794
"Sir, forgive me most unfortunate resemblance The true robber was arrested three days ago.

65
00:04:18.895 --> 00:04:19.556
You are free!"

66
00:04:20.637 --> 00:04:24.120
Phileas Fogg walked up to the detective and looked him steadily in the face.

67
00:04:25.028 --> 00:04:29.209
With the only rapid motion he ever made in his life, he drew back his arm and knocked

68
00:04:29.309 --> 00:04:30.990
Fix down with one precise punch.

69
00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.491
"Well hit!"

70
00:04:33.811 --> 00:04:34.751
cried Passepartout.

71
00:04:36.431 --> 00:04:37.552
Phileas Fogg was free!

72
00:04:38.372 --> 00:04:40.353
It was just after three o'clock, and Mr.

73
00:04:40.393 --> 00:04:42.533
Fogg was due at the Reform Club at a quarter to nine.

74
00:04:43.333 --> 00:04:46.034
They would have to make the journey to London in five and a half hours.

75
00:04:46.954 --> 00:04:51.756
Mr. Fogg immediately ordered a special train and set out for London with Aouda and his faithful servant.

76
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.021
Their last hope was to keep the train at top speed.

77
00:04:57.801 --> 00:05:01.523
This would have been easy if the rails had been clear, but other trains caused delays.

78
00:05:02.663 --> 00:05:03.244
So when Mr.

79
00:05:03.284 --> 00:05:07.926
Fogg stepped from his train at the station, all the clocks in London were striking ten minutes before

80
00:05:08.006 --> 00:05:12.307
nine. Having completed his tour of the world, he was five minutes too late!

81
00:05:13.168 --> 00:05:14.148
He had lost his wager.

82
00:05:19.971 --> 00:05:20.751
Can you believe it?

83
00:05:21.351 --> 00:05:21.751
I can't.

84
00:05:22.272 --> 00:05:23.152
I'm still in shock!

85
00:05:24.048 --> 00:05:25.169
After all that, Mr.

86
00:05:25.269 --> 00:05:28.030
Fogg lost his wager—and by only five minutes.

87
00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.551
If only we had gone faster.

88
00:05:31.672 --> 00:05:33.252
Although at the end of our trip, we rushed!

89
00:05:34.113 --> 00:05:36.934
Queenstown to Liverpool to London in just over a day!

90
00:05:38.315 --> 00:05:40.696
Queenstown is a port on the southern coast of Ireland.

91
00:05:41.536 --> 00:05:44.377
This city has had several names, including Cove and Cobh.

92
00:05:45.198 --> 00:05:49.820
It was officially renamed Queenstown about 25 years ago, after Queen Victoria visited.

93
00:05:50.608 --> 00:05:53.889
Some people think that someday the city will go back to its original name, Cobh.

94
00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.772
Liverpool, located in northwest England, is also a major seaport.

95
00:06:00.552 --> 00:06:05.294
In fact, it is sometimes called "the second port of the Empire" because it is the busiest port

96
00:06:05.334 --> 00:06:05.854
after London.

97
00:06:06.874 --> 00:06:10.196
There are many beautiful buildings here, displaying this city's impressive wealth.
